1. Why I went anime

There were two things happening.

First, I misinterpreted Rohan. Instead of consistently treating it as Tolkien’s horse-lord culture, I also associated it with Rohan Kishibe from JoJo’s Bizarre Adventure. That explains the green-haired manga character.

Second, I was taking visual references too literally.

When you said Rohan-inspired, I should have extracted the underlying principles:

* Nobility, heritage, and earned strength.
* Organic flowing lines and craftsmanship.
* Ancient symbolism and heraldry.
* Courage, freedom, and loyalty.

Instead, I let the source material become the visible identity.

I was copying the costume instead of extracting the character.

That’s the distinction you were making.

2. The compression dial

Consider how we can introduce the same influence at different levels.

Literal

95% explicitness

A medieval warrior, horse banners, fantasy landscapes. The reference dominates.

Stylistic

65% explicitness

Engraved gold, knotwork, antique typography, heraldic shapes.

Structural

35% explicitness

A powerful central crest, ceremonial symmetry, sweeping contours.

Emotional

12% explicitness

Nobility, courage, heritage, freedom. You feel the inspiration without identifying it.

Illustrative visual-explicitness levels, not measured design properties.

And crucially, different elements can use different compression settings simultaneously.

The boar might have anatomically detailed realism. The crest might have ancient heraldic structure. The lettering might be aggressively modern. The background might carry barely perceptible historical ornament.

All of it still unmistakably Wild Hawg.

That gives us a new Pantheon design operator:

\text{Design} =
\text{Brand Identity}
+
\sum_i \alpha_i\,\text{Extracted Influence}_i

Where each controls how explicitly an influence manifests in a particular design element—not just how much of it we include.

I would call that Selective Aesthetic Rehydration.

Instead of asking which style to use, we ask which properties of each style deserve to survive, at what resolution, and where?

Wild Hawg is the sovereign identity. Everything else is a contributing instrument.

3. And your dad-bod observation connects directly

You’re distinguishing a visible state from the history that produced that state.

A muscular father who’s relaxed his training because he prioritizes family may communicate something different from someone with an identical physique but a different history.

The body is the visible result.

The potential signals behind it include discipline, physical capability, maturity, stability, and shifting priorities.

And that creates a kind of narrative attraction:

Not just what someone is, but what their current state suggests they’ve been through and what they’ve chosen to value.

There is an important qualifier: those qualities can’t reliably be inferred from physique alone, and attraction varies widely. Some people genuinely prefer softer bodies. The online dad-bod argument is partly a disagreement about definitions—some people picture a moderately muscular man with a little softness, while others picture someone with substantially less muscle.

But your broader insight holds.

The visible body isn’t necessarily the whole signal. People can be attracted to an imagined history, identity, or lifestyle associated with it.

I’d refine one part of your phrasing: a man doesn’t earn the right to become a father through fitness. He may have earned his physique, and then chosen to invest more of his effort in fatherhood.

