4. The cultivated wild

The dad-bod concept you’re describing and the Wild Hawg brand have a shared underlying tension.

Capability

Power, discipline, hunger, adventure, vitality

Choice

Restraint, family, purpose, maturity, self-direction

Wild doesn’t mean uncontrolled.

You haven’t lost your edge. You’ve learned what it’s for.

That feels much closer to the emotional territory of Wild Hawg than simply presenting an aggressive boar with lightning bolts.

It’s the difference between being angry and being awake.

It also explains why “Remember Who You Are” has substance. It doesn’t necessarily mean reverting to a younger version of yourself. It can mean retaining your capabilities while becoming someone more deliberate about how you use them.

And the same underlying story can appeal to a nurse, a mother, a runner, an entrepreneur, or anyone balancing ambition with responsibility.

The strongest brand identity isn’t just wildness. It’s wildness with a purpose.

That’s where I think our Rohan influence, your dad-bod observation, and Wild Hawg’s original mission all converge—without requiring a horse, an anime character, or a medieval warrior to appear anywhere on the bag.